"comparison": "Both Citi and JPMorgan provide multi-seller commercial paper conduits with liquidity support consisting of deal-specific facilities and program-wide backstops. Citi describes these as transaction-specific asset purchase agreements (APAs) to purchase receivables at par, and short-term lending commitments for market disruptions. JPMorgan similarly provides a minimum 100% deal-specific liquidity facility and uncommitted program-wide liquidity/credit enhancement. A key similarity is that both firms provide these facilities to support commercial paper issuance for clients. However, Citi explicitly notes that funding under its APA may subject clients to increased interest costs, whereas JPMorgan's disclosure focuses on the existence of the facilities to ensure timely repayment without quantifying the cost impact on clients in this specific section.",
